    "q-217272": {
      "dom_id": "step-q217272",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The solution to $-\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2}{6t}$ is$\\,\\,t\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $t=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{2}{6t} \\\\ \\frac{-4}{3} & =\\frac{2}{6t} \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 6t & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\ -24t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24t & =6 \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{-24} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is different from $t=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $t=-\\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
